% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1D, for what was read).
% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1), the earliest notation with every construct used: arithmetic (c + 1) and key (docs/decisions.md rules 21, 23).
% IDIOM: a relation over the items of a list, with key so that it waits for the list: Foster and Elcock 1969, map (examples/map.ab1: map'=lambda p, f key p begin {null(p) or p = [r'&s'] f(r) map(s,f)} end; docs/decisions.md rule 21);
% IDIOM: a case's head as equations on the lambda's parameters: Elcock 1990 p. 8 (app, examples/append.abs);
% IDIOM: lists [a;b] and [h&t]: Foster and Elcock 1969 (tutorial ch. 8, ch. 10);
% IDIOM: arithmetic as an assertion usable in every direction: Foster and Elcock 1969 (tutorial ch. 8); Foster 1968, pass sets.
% P14b. Unknowns r1, r2. One stage: the counter receives the sequence <INC, INC, READ(r1), INC, READ(r2)>, run(m, 0).
% The message INC is the atom 'inc'; READ(r) is the list ['read';r] (Absys has no other compound term).
% run is P14a.ab1's text, unchanged.
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
inc1 = 'inc'   % stage 1: the first message <atom> (INC)
inc2 = 'inc'   % stage 1: the second message <atom> (INC)
inc4 = 'inc'   % stage 1: the fourth message <atom> (INC)
% CONSTANTS END
% run, P14 Vocabulary, cases 1, 2 and 3 in that order, each head as equations on the parameters.
% Case 2: run(<INC> ++ s, c) if run(s, c + 1), c + 1 as a fresh unknown. Case 3: run(<READ(r)> ++ s, c) if r = c and run(s, c).
% key p (C): run waits until its list of messages has a first cell, instead of making lists of every length (FE69 map; rule 21).
run'=lambda p, c key p
    begin {p = [] or
           p = ['inc'&u'] c1' = c + 1 run(u, c1) or
           p = [['read';v']&w'] v = c run(w, c)} end
% STAGE 1
run([inc1;inc2;['read';r1];inc4;['read';r2]], 0)
